00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.139
And it is very real.

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.770
And it's very much in use.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.410
And so what is machine learning?

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:17.930
Now the ideal of artificial
intelligence is not new.

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.960
It's been around since the
early days of computing.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:25.780
And it's captivated computer
scientists the early 1980s.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.230
This idea of you don't
have to tell the computer

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:31.000
that everything it
needs to know up front

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.160
by being able to build
these machines that

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.055
know how to learn.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:39.640
They know how to make
observations and do inferences

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.790
based on these observations
has been a field of computer

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.610
science for a long time.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.990
Now on the field of deep
learning and particular,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.460
is a under field of
machine learning.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.490
And it's got a lot of general
use in computer science.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:03:00.500
So it's a field that we at
Google look at very closely.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.750
And this is how it works.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:04.460
Think about how you learned
your very first language.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.820
You know you didn't sit
there with a grammar

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.460
book and a dictionary.

00:03:09.460 --> 00:03:12.640
You sat there with your
parents and your surroundings.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.300
And you call the computer a
computer, you got a thumbs up.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.120
If you called it a book,
they said no, no, no.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.260
It's a computer.

00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:24.880
So this idea of examples and
repeated practice and learning

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:28.100
is the same idea in
machine learning as well.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.460
So in machine learning,
we expose a system

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.840
to many, many, many examples.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.620
And those examples are
what teachers the machine.

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.140
Who here has played
Atari Breakout?

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:44.170
Any early Atari
Breakout players?

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.510
OK, there's a few.

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:50.950
So here is one where we
taught a neural network

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:53.950
to play Atari Breakout.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.640
After 10 minutes of training,
it's pretty much random luck.

00:03:57.640 --> 00:03:59.320
You The paddle's moving.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.650
It's try to hit it,
but it's a hit or miss.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:07.420
Now this is what happened
after two hours of play.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.140
Now it's gotten pretty good.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:11.200
It's not missing anything.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.630
It's almost an expert.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.830
Now watch this.

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.720
This is what happens
after four hours.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.630
Just so you know,
the expert players

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:23.970
will know the way to win
in this game is you drill

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.490
a little tunnel through
the side and you

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:27.930
try to trap the ball there.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.670
And that's how you
get the extra points.

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.920
So not only is the
computer learning,

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:35.250
but it is now building strategy.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.860
And it's doing this
phenomenally fast.

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:41.280
After four hours, a human
being couldn't get this fast,

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.100
but obviously the
machine is there.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:45.090
So why now?

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.940
Why are we talking about
machine learning today?

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.560
Why is it becoming such a hype?

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.500
Well, a successful
machine learning

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:54.720
has three things that it needs.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.960
It is a good model
and powerful tools.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:02.080
It needs lots of data
and good quality data.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:04.830
And it needs lots of
computational power.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.640
Now the models we've had,
we've had them for a while.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.630
So the last two is now
what we're able to do.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:17.850
Because now things like cloud is
able to give us massive amounts

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.450
of storage and on cost
effective computational power.

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:24.280
So we were able to do
pretty interesting things.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.800
So let's look at how
we at Google have

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.280
been using machine learning.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:32.220
So let's look at some examples.

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.040
So this is from our
source code repository.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:38.800
These are the machine learning
models that we have today.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.840
Now, if you look at 2012,
it's very little, very few.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.800
Now you look at today, that's
an exponential curve that you're

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:47.550
looking at.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.750
The growth is massive in
the way we internally are

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.240
making use of machine learning.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:59.050
Who here has used Smart Reply?

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.540
Any users of Smart Reply?

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:04.420
So if you haven't, check
it out, because it's going

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:08.122
to save you a lot of time.

00:06:08.122 --> 00:06:09.580
The first time we
did it, we did it

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:11.170
as an April Fools Day's joke.

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:13.210
We thought it was
never going to happen,

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:14.950
at least not in the short term.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:19.510
But then we actually made
it a product in 2015.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:25.375
And today, 10% of all email
replies come from this.

00:06:25.375 --> 00:06:28.330
This just basically says
here are some sample

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:29.602
replies that you can send.

00:06:29.602 --> 00:06:31.060
And all you have
to do is hit send.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:35.140
The machine guesses
from the contacts.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:39.970
Google Photos-- so recently,
my family was on a vacation.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:41.470
We went to the beach.

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:43.930
And my mom asked
for some photos.

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:45.110
And we had hundreds--

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.350
I think we had 400,
500 pictures in there.

00:06:48.350 --> 00:06:50.830
And I was looking for the
one to send to my mom.

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:54.790
So I looked-- I said
Southampton, beach, waves, Evan

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.040
and Ryan, the names of my boys.

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.350
And I was able to get
the photo instantly

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.570
without having to go
through all those photos.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:03.910
Machine learning was
able to help me do that.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:09.200
And we have a very happy
grandmother at the end.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:11.280
And our data centers--

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.730
so we pay a lot of
attention to the power usage

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:15.760
of our data centers.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.570
It's very important to us.

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:20.580
And it's not just because of the
cost, it's also because of what

00:07:20.580 --> 00:07:22.120
the environment.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.590
Now in our data centers,
we've built a lot of ways

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:28.150
to build super
efficient hardware.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:31.950
We also looked at efficient
ways to pool our data centers

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.140
in some crazy ways.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.170
And we invested heavily
in green energy.

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:41.010
Because we want to be 100%
powered by renewable energy

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:43.080
by the end of this year.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.684
The real interesting thing is
we sent our DeepMind engineers

00:07:47.684 --> 00:07:48.850
to look at our data centers.

00:07:48.850 --> 00:07:52.000
We've been collecting a lot
of data from our data centers.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:54.960
Now the complication with
them is obviously the way

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.930
the equipment works and the
environmental factors work.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.970
And you know how everything
is, it's not a linear system.

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:05.010
So it's very hard to maximize it
looking at the traditional ways

00:08:05.010 --> 00:08:07.080
of heuristic modeling.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.840
So by doing that, looking
at things like sensors

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.690
from server racks, chillers,
and cooling towers,

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.330
and temperatures, and
pump speeds and weather,

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:16.940
all sorts of factors--

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.080
I think we looked at
maybe 120 of them--

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:24.510
we were able to bring down our
costs, cooling costs by 40%.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.834
We did that and then
we didn't believe it.

00:08:26.834 --> 00:08:27.750
We said wait a minute.

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.250
That sounds a little
too good to be true.

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:34.020
So when we did it, it
just jumped down by 40%.

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.720
So we said could
this be really true?

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.150
And we flipped it off and
it went right back up.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:42.000
So then we knew it was for real.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:42.835
It actually worked.

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:47.980
AlphaGo-- I think many
people in this room

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:49.780
are familiar with
AlphaGo, so I'm not

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:51.100
going to go too much into it.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.530
But I do want to make one point.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:57.430
I think if you remember
when IBM's Deep Blue beat

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:00.470
Kasparov, that was a big deal.

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:04.810
That was a game of chess that
was very hard to model prior.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:08.830
But if you look at the
complexity of a game like chess

00:09:08.830 --> 00:09:11.020
and AlphaGo and
make a comparison,

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:14.350
the number of possible
moves in a chess game

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:17.890
is equal to the number
of atoms in a human body.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:21.250
The number of possible
moves in AlphaGo

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:25.700
is equal to the number
of atoms in the universe.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.910
So it's a problem
that you can't just

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:31.000
solve by brute
computational power.

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:32.340
It's just not possible.

00:09:32.340 --> 00:09:34.420
Well, maybe we get
quantum computers,

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.920
and I know it will be possible,
but today it's not possible.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.400
So to do this, AlphaGo
had to build intuition.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:44.530
Remember we looked at
the Atari Breakout game,

00:09:44.530 --> 00:09:46.600
and that was about
building strategy.

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:49.930
In AlphaGo it was about
building intuition.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:52.090
And I think that's
the thing I think

00:09:52.090 --> 00:09:54.940
that's very impressive about
what our researchers were

00:09:54.940 --> 00:09:56.420
able to do with AlphaGo.

00:09:59.910 --> 00:10:02.780
So my mother, the same
mother that I sent

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:05.490
spent the pictures
to, she's a diabetic.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.260
And she has been diabetic
for a very long time.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:12.450
And there is about
400 million people

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:17.040
today in the world that
are at risk of going blind.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:19.590
They're at risk of going blind
because of the very, very

00:10:19.590 --> 00:10:21.510
simple illness.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:24.030
And you really need two
things to treat this.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:27.510
You need the retinal
scan of the eye.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.480
And you need a very, very
highly trained specialist

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.670
to be able to look
at that retinal scan

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.930
to make the diagnosis.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.660
And then the
treatments super easy.

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:39.540
Now of course the problem
is in many, many parts

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.110
of the world like
Africa or in India,

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:45.060
even part of United
States and I think Europe,

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:48.570
we do not have those
highly trained specialists.

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:51.330
So our researchers and
the Google Brain team

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.640
worked with physicians.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:56.460
And what they were
able to do is apply

00:10:56.460 --> 00:11:00.900
machine learning to this
particular eye disease where

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:04.930
the machine today can
diagnose this image,

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:08.660
at least as good as
highly trained physicians.

00:11:08.660 --> 00:11:12.240
And I'm very thankful
that for personal matters,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.300
but also for humanity.

00:11:16.300 --> 00:11:17.295
So take a look at this.

00:11:22.940 --> 00:11:25.730
So this is not me running,
although I do sometimes

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:26.550
run like that.

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:28.410
But this is a humanoid.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:31.430
This is a humanoid
figure that was

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:35.300
trained by our DeepMind people.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:39.290
And what they've done is they
didn't tell the humanoids

00:11:39.290 --> 00:11:42.830
to virtual characters how to
walk or how to make progress.

00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:44.460
They didn't teach them anything.

00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:48.920
All they did was they gave them
virtual sensors that meant that

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:51.980
they would know if they
were upward or not--

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:53.540
where are they standing up--

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:55.850
or were they making
forward progress.

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:58.580
So the only thing
they were told to do

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:00.750
was to keep going forward.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:04.760
So everything here, the jumping,
the limboing, the leaping,

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:08.330
it was everything that the
computer devised itself

00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:15.470
as a way to get from A to B.
And the rest of it, the machine

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:16.030
did itself.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:24.320
So if this was 2014, and
I was standing up here

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:28.040
and I asked all of you to
take your cell phones out,

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.810
and if everybody in the
world did the same thing

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:34.310
and we talked to our assistants
for about three minutes,

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:37.090
we would have completely
crashed Google.

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:40.970
It would go completely depleted
all the processing power.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:43.800
Now we were doing some
really cool things,

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:48.120
but we were using a lot,
a lot of processing power.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:52.190
So we decided to build our
own machine learning hardware.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:55.400
So we built these things
called TensorFlow processing

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:57.350
units, TPUs.

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:00.470
Now there's two very interesting
properties of neural nets

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:02.420
and learning algorithms.

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:04.520
Property number one
is they don't need

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:06.650
as much precision in the math.

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:09.230
We don't need to
keep the n-th digit

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:12.080
in our mathematical
calculations.

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:16.130
The other thing is we only
need a handful of operations

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.470
that we really need
to do really fast.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:20.950
And we don't need
the genericness,

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:25.310
the generality of the
general purpose computers.

00:13:25.310 --> 00:13:27.950
So by using those
two properties,

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:30.380
we built these chips
which now allow

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:34.520
us to accelerate neural
network computations

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.530
by an order of magnitude.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:41.180
And these are available today to
use in Google Cloud Platforms.

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:48.210
So it's not just Google doing
pretty amazing things today.

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:50.850
Our clients that
are using Google

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.710
are also doing some
really interesting things.

00:13:53.710 --> 00:13:55.590
So let's take a look
at a few of them.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:00.100
Any parents here?

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:01.520
Any parents?

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:02.990
Anyone with babies?

00:14:02.990 --> 00:14:05.700
Or remember were
babies were like?

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:10.980
QP is a Japanese food producer,
and they make baby food.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.730
And remember when our
babies were young,

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.760
we were very nervous about
what they were eating?

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:20.150
So when QP is making baby
food, every now and then

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:22.770
you get this discolored
pieces of potatoes.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:24.860
And they're perfectly
fine to consume,

00:14:24.860 --> 00:14:27.620
but if you are a nervous
parent with your first baby

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:30.980
in your arms, you don't want
that in your baby's food.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:35.420
So what QP was allowed to do
is they used Google's machine

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:37.890
learning algorithms to train.

00:14:37.890 --> 00:14:41.000
And they used about
18,000 photos as input

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:44.690
to this process to
automatically identify and get

00:14:44.690 --> 00:14:49.190
rid of the discolored pieces
of potatoes in the baby food

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:52.650
to have more happier and
more content parents.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:58.160
Ocado-- anyone
familiar with Ocado?

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:02.540
They are UK online
grocer and they have

00:15:02.540 --> 00:15:05.400
about 500,000 active customers.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:09.320
They're the world's largest
online only grocery store.

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:12.420
Because they only operate
in the online space,

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.120
there is only two ways that they
interact with their clients.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.290
It's their ordering system,
where you go and make

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:21.341
your grocery shopping, and
it's their call center.

00:15:21.341 --> 00:15:22.340
Those are the only ways.

00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:24.870
They don't have a human
to human interaction.

00:15:24.870 --> 00:15:27.350
So it's super important
for them to be

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:30.660
able to make the most of this
human computer interaction.

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:34.760
So their clients can call them,
tweet them, or email them.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:38.030
So Ocado uses the Google
Cloud Platform and our machine

00:15:38.030 --> 00:15:41.900
learning products to basically
prioritize and segment all

00:15:41.900 --> 00:15:45.650
these incoming requests so that
the urgent request, something

00:15:45.650 --> 00:15:48.260
that has to do with the quality
of your immediate delivery,

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:52.070
gets answered first, rather than
someone just sending an email

00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:55.160
to thank them for their service,
which they get an email back

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:56.720
to, but it's not as immediate.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.350
So using machine learning to
understand context and what's

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:03.560
happening in these
emails and tweets

00:16:03.560 --> 00:16:06.230
and calls to be able to
serve their customers better.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.510
Marine sanctuaries--
all over the world

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:17.180
marine sanctuaries are at risk
today due to illegal fishing.

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:21.560
Global Fishing Watch
aims to combat this.

00:16:21.560 --> 00:16:24.410
Now one of the
problems though, they

00:16:24.410 --> 00:16:27.690
get about $400 billion
location messages.

00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:31.400
They get all GPS messages that
tells them where the fishing

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.010
vessels are, which is great.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:36.560
You can know where all
the fishing vessels are.

00:16:36.560 --> 00:16:38.690
The problem is
looking at this, you

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:42.170
can't tell if this is
just a vehicle passing by

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:45.650
or somebody doing
illegal fishing.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:47.000
It's hard to do.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:51.670
So it turns out that
you can tell them apart

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:53.830
by their moving patterns.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:56.500
You can see here the trawlers
has a very different way

00:16:56.500 --> 00:16:59.420
of moving than long lines.

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:02.830
So by looking at not
just the GPS signals,

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:08.770
but how they move
as they traverse,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:10.540
they can actually
tell them apart.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:15.770
So what they were able to do
by looking at these patterns,

00:17:15.770 --> 00:17:17.920
they helped the
government of Chapati.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.800
The government of Chapati
is this tiny little island.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:27.520
They helped them to get
fines from illegal fishers

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:30.580
to the tune of $2.2 million.

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:32.500
To us, maybe that's
not a big money.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:35.660
But that's about 1%
of their entire GDP.

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:39.740
So this was pretty impressive
what they were able to do.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:43.840
The Cardus Labs-- one of
the things that they do

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:47.200
is satellite image processing.

00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:49.450
Now this year what
they were able to do

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:51.730
was looking at
satellite images, they

00:17:51.730 --> 00:17:54.670
predicted us corn production.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:59.530
And they did that with
1.9% accuracy compared

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:02.230
to the USDA's final report.

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:05.650
What's really impressive is
they did this five months

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.110
before the actual
report came out.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.150
Five months, that's
the advantage

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:11.920
that they were able to have.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:13.900
They didn't have to wait
for the public report.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:15.400
They looked at the
satellite images.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:19.550
And they did it for themselves
and pretty accurately.

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:22.430
So machine learning
is very real and it

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:27.890
is making a very real impact
across all the industries.

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.650
Anyone here from manufacturing?

00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:31.610
Manufacturing?

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:34.220
Are you guys using
machine learning?

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:36.030
So this is what your
competitors are doing.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.280
They're doing
predictive maintenance.

00:18:38.280 --> 00:18:41.210
They're doing the
man forecasting.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:42.880
Anyone from retail?

00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:44.570
Any retailers here?

00:18:44.570 --> 00:18:45.120
There you go.

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:47.682
Are you using machine learning?

00:18:47.682 --> 00:18:48.890
Let's see what they're doing.

00:18:48.890 --> 00:18:50.330
Inventory planning?

00:18:50.330 --> 00:18:52.730
Recommendation engines?

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:57.179
Anyone using machine learning
here in their businesses?

00:18:57.179 --> 00:18:57.720
You're using.

00:18:57.720 --> 00:19:00.360
What industry are you from?

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.400
Telecommunications, that's
not even on the list.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:05.450
Well done.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:09.520
Anyone else representing
other industries?

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.310
Media, of course media.

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:15.160
You guys are way ahead
of everyone anyway.

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:15.910
Anyone else?

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:18.490
Anyone else doing
representative?

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:20.240
Financials, thank you.

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:22.460
Any one of these on this list?

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.610
Risk management, I used
to do risk management.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:26.970
Perfect.

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:29.480
So it's across everything.

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.820
It's across everything,
across every industry

00:19:31.820 --> 00:19:33.055
that we're seeing it.

00:19:33.055 --> 00:19:35.180
And this is a list taking
from our clients that are

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:37.490
doing this on our platforms.

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:42.744
So how can you get
started with this?

00:19:42.744 --> 00:19:44.160
How can you get
started with this?

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:47.540
Now everything that we have and
everything that our client does

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:50.210
is underneath it all is
powered by TensorFlow.

00:19:50.210 --> 00:19:52.740
TensorFlow is our machine
learning algorithm.

00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:56.120
It was originally developed
by our Google Brain team.

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:58.940
And in 2015 we open sourced it.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:02.760
We open sourced it under
the Apache license.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:04.370
And there's a
couple ways that you

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:06.140
can use TensorFlow yourself.

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:09.680
One, you can use the
fully trained models.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.930
I think you've seen some
examples of these keynote

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:15.680
this morning, like our Video
Intelligence API and Vision

00:20:15.680 --> 00:20:16.620
API.

00:20:16.620 --> 00:20:18.750
And there is absolutely
no learning required.

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:21.290
You can use these
out of the box.

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:23.060
The other way that
you can use it

00:20:23.060 --> 00:20:26.724
is either by using TensorFlow
machine learning libraries.

00:20:26.724 --> 00:20:28.890
And you can use these--
because they're open source,

00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:29.931
you can use them on prem.

00:20:29.931 --> 00:20:31.310
You can use it on your laptop.

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:32.930
You can use it anywhere.

00:20:32.930 --> 00:20:35.600
Or you can use our Cloud
Machine Learning Engine,

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:38.270
which is our fully
managed platform, where

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:40.940
you can attach interesting
things like TPUs,

00:20:40.940 --> 00:20:43.370
like GPUs, hardware
accelerators.

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:44.930
And because it's
fully managed, you

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:47.720
don't really have to worry
about installations and cores.

00:20:47.720 --> 00:20:50.030
It scales on its own
to be able to do this.

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:55.800
So machine learning
is not a hype.

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.870
It's not a hype.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:01.320
It looks like there's a lot
of hype, but it is very real.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.400
And it is very real
for us at Google.

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:06.330
And I think it is very
real for our customers that

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:09.660
are doing some amazing
things on our platforms.

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:12.450
So we would love to
hear more from you

00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:15.330
and hear more from
your examples.

00:21:15.330 --> 00:21:18.960
And we would love to
take you on this journey

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:21.600
to machine learning as well.

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:23.114
Thank you.

00:21:23.114 --> 00:21:29.309
