David is leading
our relationship

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.950
with the startup system.

00:21:51.950 --> 00:21:53.700
And he's going to
talk to us about how

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.670
to innovate a startup.

00:21:56.670 --> 00:22:01.071
So David, welcome on stage.

00:22:01.071 --> 00:22:02.860
DAVID ROLDAN: Thank
you, Gregoire.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.510
Hi, everyone.

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:05.980
Great to be here today.

00:22:05.980 --> 00:22:09.190
So as Gregoire mentioned,
I lead all our startup

00:22:09.190 --> 00:22:13.330
and investor engagement across
all of Europe for the cloud

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:14.210
organization.

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:15.740
So what does that mean?

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:17.860
It means that I also
run our startup program.

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:22.290
So we use that as a mechanism
to help companies to accelerate

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:23.740
and scale and grow.

00:22:23.740 --> 00:22:27.350
And we're always looking
for different trends.

00:22:27.350 --> 00:22:27.850
What works?

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:28.480
What doesn't?

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:31.030
What do the most
successful people that

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:32.560
are building what's next do?

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.142
What are the commonalities?

00:22:34.142 --> 00:22:36.100
So today we'll talk about
some of those themes,

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:38.830
because the reality is
a lot of those themes

00:22:38.830 --> 00:22:41.080
and a lot of the
underlying principles

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:45.370
can be used across virtually any
industry, any size a company,

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:46.690
larger or small.

00:22:46.690 --> 00:22:48.170
So we'll talk about that.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:52.900
So first, I want to start
with just a concept.

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:57.730
So we'll talk for
instance, Lee methodology,

00:22:57.730 --> 00:22:59.980
widely known, widely used.

00:22:59.980 --> 00:23:02.800
But many startups
are often taught

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:06.054
to be able to follow some
sort of the methodology

00:23:06.054 --> 00:23:07.720
for the purpose of
making sure they have

00:23:07.720 --> 00:23:10.397
a good strong feedback loop
for the purpose of trying

00:23:10.397 --> 00:23:11.980
to make sure that
they understand what

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:15.430
their customers are saying and
ultimately, how do you iterate

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:18.820
and be able to get
features out there quicker?

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:20.740
A lot of it is simply
based upon the fact

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:24.640
that you have a limited amount
of resources, time, money,

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:25.490
people.

00:23:25.490 --> 00:23:30.300
So that means that you've got
to be smart in how you operate.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:33.750
The reality is most
of us fall into this.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:36.600
And it doesn't matter
what size company you are.

00:23:36.600 --> 00:23:38.850
The hard part is being
able to come up for air

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.350
and be able to
understand what is it

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:41.730
that our customers are saying?

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:43.530
And then how do
we prioritize what

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:45.690
they're say that's going
to yield us the greatest

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.190
value on the other side?

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:50.520
So a lot of that is
trying to figure out

00:23:50.520 --> 00:23:52.789
how do we steer away from this?

00:23:52.789 --> 00:23:55.080
How do we make sure that
we're operating as efficiently

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.370
as possible?

00:23:56.370 --> 00:23:59.070
Because what we don't want
is for this to happen.

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:02.370
And I always loved this
picture, because the symbolism,

00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:04.560
it just means so much.

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:06.180
From a startup
perspective it's hey,

00:24:06.180 --> 00:24:08.580
are we effectively
managing burn rate?

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:12.300
From a large organization,
maybe it's multimillion budget.

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:15.360
But doesn't make it any better
if it's been put to waste.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:17.770
And nothing meaningful
was achieved.

00:24:17.770 --> 00:24:21.630
So this starts to have a shift
in how people are thinking

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:24.960
and the considerations that
they make, from hey, let's

00:24:24.960 --> 00:24:28.830
try and achieve the holy grail
of continuous integration,

00:24:28.830 --> 00:24:30.150
continuous delivery.

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:32.730
Because we want to be able to
iterate as quickly as possible,

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:35.670
get those features out
there to delight customers.

00:24:35.670 --> 00:24:40.020
So that in itself is
easier said than done.

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:41.400
Many of us feel like this.

00:24:41.400 --> 00:24:44.010
Certainly mind of
an entrepreneur.

00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:45.690
Many can say the
barrier of entry

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:48.270
to start something new
has never been easier,

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:49.470
certainly with cloud.

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:52.770
But then the reality is kind of
going from that first million

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.690
dollar revenue to $10
million to $100 million,

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.023
et cetera has never been harder.

00:24:56.023 --> 00:24:59.400
And many of you that
work and operate

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:00.990
in organizations
that have been around

00:25:00.990 --> 00:25:04.540
for a very long time, that
can be a challenging thing.

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:07.590
There's a lot more data inputs,
a lot more considerations

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:10.810
that need to be taken
into account for.

00:25:10.810 --> 00:25:14.850
So a lot of that can be kind
of synonymous with noise.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:21.570
But the reality is, all the talk
about machine learning and AI,

00:25:21.570 --> 00:25:24.600
it's wonderful and
Ulku is correct.

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:25.840
It's not a fad.

00:25:25.840 --> 00:25:28.590
It's something that's built
to last and will be around.

00:25:28.590 --> 00:25:32.580
But none of it means
anything without data.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:33.930
Data trumps all.

00:25:33.930 --> 00:25:36.240
And if you think
about most of the work

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:38.490
people do when it comes
to machine learning,

00:25:38.490 --> 00:25:43.800
probably about 90% of that
work is about pre-processing,

00:25:43.800 --> 00:25:45.090
data cleansing.

00:25:45.090 --> 00:25:47.820
And only probably
about 10% and actually

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:49.620
trying to get those insights.

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:53.174
So you start to see a trend
of people that ultimately

00:25:53.174 --> 00:25:54.840
are trying to figure
out how do we solve

00:25:54.840 --> 00:25:56.640
for a lot of these challenges?

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:58.560
And they will apply
things like machine

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:02.190
learning to be able to
make sense of it all.

00:26:02.190 --> 00:26:06.580
Because the reality is
there's a talent war

00:26:06.580 --> 00:26:08.830
that exists out there in
trying to find people that

00:26:08.830 --> 00:26:10.990
can help you be more agile.

00:26:10.990 --> 00:26:13.000
And that's where
you start seeing

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:16.720
a lot more of the kind
of human resources

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:19.270
related startups being born.

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:21.010
Because it's hard
to find talent.

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:22.720
Where did these people exist?

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.820
If you consider that there's--

00:26:24.820 --> 00:26:27.310
when I last looked--
there's about 21 million

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:29.170
professional coders
in the world.

00:26:29.170 --> 00:26:32.310
How many of those are
deep learning engineers?

00:26:32.310 --> 00:26:34.630
There's a lot less of those.

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:37.930
And then you start to have a
lot of this philosophical type

00:26:37.930 --> 00:26:40.840
of conversations of who do we
want to be as an organization?

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:44.080
What type of culture do we want
to kind of have and instill?

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:46.840
Most companies have been
very centralized in how

00:26:46.840 --> 00:26:48.460
they group and operate.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:51.340
But now trying to figure
out how do we inject AI

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:53.110
into some of our products?

00:26:53.110 --> 00:26:55.330
Because we think
it will massively

00:26:55.330 --> 00:26:59.287
improve how people operate
and delight customers.

00:26:59.287 --> 00:27:01.120
Now you start thinking
about well let's hire

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:02.680
people wherever they are.

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:05.190
So all of a sudden
it becomes well,

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:06.790
are we have fragmented
organization

00:27:06.790 --> 00:27:09.730
or it's just simply we're
become a bit more decentralized.

00:27:09.730 --> 00:27:11.540
So new conversations being had.

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:14.085
And startups across the
globe are faced with this,

00:27:14.085 --> 00:27:15.460
especially because
they're always

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.090
faced with limited resources.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:18.640
So they are trying
to find the best.

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:21.840
And then it's also how
do you retain the talent?

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:25.000
And for many of us, that can
be just as hard as finding

00:27:25.000 --> 00:27:27.370
someone, if not harder.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:29.410
As they say, good
people are hard to find.

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:31.250
So then you start
seeing new type

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:34.100
of companies being
born like PyCon

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:36.660
who's a Danish startup company.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:38.800
Ultimately they
try and synthesize

00:27:38.800 --> 00:27:41.110
all the kind of
data input of what

00:27:41.110 --> 00:27:43.300
are our employees telling us?

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:45.730
And then trying to
make use of that data

00:27:45.730 --> 00:27:49.330
so employees can figure out how
do we take the proper action?

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:52.000
And what's going to yield
us the greatest result?

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:57.280
Then beyond that, there's
also revenue and users.

00:27:57.280 --> 00:27:59.320
Many of us, that's
why we're here.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:02.560
Trying to figure out how
do we acquire more people?

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:04.810
How do we continue to grow?

00:28:04.810 --> 00:28:08.590
And it's no different
for any type of customer.

00:28:08.590 --> 00:28:12.520
But internally, we often talk
about the next billion users.

00:28:12.520 --> 00:28:13.695
What does that mean?

00:28:13.695 --> 00:28:14.570
How do you find them?

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:15.760
How do you attract them?

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.820
And really, that kind of is
synonymous with the last point,

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:19.910
which is data.

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.910
Data is the de facto king.

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:23.590
It's the currency
that matters most.

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:25.631
Because it's how you're
going to get the greatest

00:28:25.631 --> 00:28:28.360
amount of insights, and that's
where you see a lot of people

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:31.270
gravitating more towards
trying to understand their data

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:34.390
pipelines and the
analytics pieces

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.340
to be able to kind of
make sense of it all.

00:28:39.320 --> 00:28:41.720
In the past, I suppose
you could say, certainly

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:45.860
when I guess the internet first
kind of came of age that it was

00:28:45.860 --> 00:28:49.670
much easier to
try and understand

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:52.970
what is it that we need to
know about our customers.

00:28:52.970 --> 00:28:57.350
Now I think you can argue that
it's never been more complex.

00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:00.120
Because now we want
to know everything.

00:29:00.120 --> 00:29:02.180
Because it is about data.

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:06.950
And making one change
here or a slight variable

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:10.340
could have a much larger
impact than we would otherwise

00:29:10.340 --> 00:29:11.330
have known.

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:14.840
So now it's much more about
how do we make sense of it?

00:29:14.840 --> 00:29:17.480
But this is where
you start to see

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:20.300
a lot of that
shift going into we

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:21.980
want to get into
predictive analytics

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:23.139
or predictive behavior.

00:29:23.139 --> 00:29:25.430
That's when we start getting
into the machine learning.

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:29.330
And with that though,
I do caution people.

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:32.000
Because it's very easy
to say, hey, we're

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:33.530
going to go down
that rabbit hole.

00:29:33.530 --> 00:29:37.850
And we're going to build a
machine learning capable team.

00:29:37.850 --> 00:29:41.540
We're going to hire a bunch
of data science engineers.

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:46.460
But one of the kind of
biggest fallacies, or I

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:48.920
guess one of the biggest
mistakes I often see people do

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:51.320
is they didn't
understand the use case.

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:53.690
Because more often
than not, the use case

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:56.120
for people that want
to do machine learning,

00:29:56.120 --> 00:29:58.910
there's something that they
could use that's off the shelf,

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:00.650
pre-trained API.

00:30:00.650 --> 00:30:03.470
So there's been a lot
of talk about it today

00:30:03.470 --> 00:30:04.880
between the key
note and whatnot.

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:10.770
But certainly natural language,
OCR, Vision API, speech text,

00:30:10.770 --> 00:30:13.910
et cetera, a lot of those are
being leveraged and utilized.

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:18.380
Now that said, every now and
then we hit a fork in the road

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:23.420
where it actually makes sense
to go and build your own models.

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:25.890
But I do urge you to make sure
that you understand that use

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:26.390
case.

00:30:26.390 --> 00:30:29.450
Because if there are things
that you can use, great.

00:30:29.450 --> 00:30:31.830
That way you don't have to
incur a lot of technical debt.

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:34.610
But if you do have
to go out and embark

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:37.400
on building your own,
then the top two questions

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:38.450
we always get--

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:42.140
certainly I always get from
startups and investors is--

00:30:42.140 --> 00:30:44.017
how do we get
access to data sets?

00:30:44.017 --> 00:30:45.350
And how do we hire these people?

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:46.740
What do we find them?

00:30:46.740 --> 00:30:49.790
So there's many
ways to go about it.

00:30:49.790 --> 00:30:53.870
Kaggle is a wonderful way
to be able to try and solve

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:55.550
for those things.

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:58.010
In the interest of
transparency, Google

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:00.470
did acquire Kaggle
in March this year.

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:02.570
But I think what
the magic of Kaggle

00:31:02.570 --> 00:31:05.840
is that it's effectively a
data science marketplace.

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:08.480
And people will post
competitions there.

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:11.690
And I always tell people
if you're looking to hire,

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:13.310
then post the competition.

00:31:13.310 --> 00:31:16.520
The people that can solve
the problem, the challenge,

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:17.554
go hire them.

00:31:17.554 --> 00:31:19.220
There will be other
people that are just

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:20.875
kind of talking about it.

00:31:20.875 --> 00:31:24.940
They're probably full of
it and don't hire them.

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:27.992
And then routinely
Kaggle and people

00:31:27.992 --> 00:31:29.450
that contribute to
that marketplace

00:31:29.450 --> 00:31:32.720
are posting new datasets
virtually every day.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:35.480
And I think now with
Google acquiring them,

00:31:35.480 --> 00:31:37.620
we're going to be
pushing even more there.

00:31:37.620 --> 00:31:41.900
So Dr. [INAUDIBLE],,
who heads up kind

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:43.910
of machine learning
within Google Cloud,

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:47.240
quite often she's always talking
about the democratization

00:31:47.240 --> 00:31:48.410
of AI.

00:31:48.410 --> 00:31:50.120
She's a firm believer in that.

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.320
And I think that's a
very important thing.

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:54.380
And I think that's why
you'll start to continue

00:31:54.380 --> 00:31:57.180
to see us go down that path.

00:31:57.180 --> 00:32:01.010
And I think that's a good
natural segue to what is yet

00:32:01.010 --> 00:32:03.930
another trend that you see
a lot of startups using,

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:07.810
but how does that relate
to the greater population?

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:10.850
A lot of that is open
source technologies.

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:13.560
Now more than ever, people are
building from the ground up

00:32:13.560 --> 00:32:15.360
using open source.

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:19.080
And there's a variety
of reasons for that.

00:32:19.080 --> 00:32:21.200
Some of it just
from a pure ability

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.220
to maintain costs
more effectively.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:27.410
But then an even bigger
emphasis is largely

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:30.740
on the speed to innovation,
the power the community.

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:35.090
So when I think
about Kubernetes,

00:32:35.090 --> 00:32:38.220
I think the latest release
1.8 came out a few weeks ago.

00:32:38.220 --> 00:32:40.960
But when I think
about the 1.7 release,

00:32:40.960 --> 00:32:43.250
I think it was only a
few months prior to that.

00:32:43.250 --> 00:32:45.208
So there's something to
be said about the power

00:32:45.208 --> 00:32:47.450
of the community and
that acceleration.

00:32:47.450 --> 00:32:50.750
And the top two open
source projects on GitHub

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:53.780
are Kubernetes and TensorFlow,
both Google projects.

00:32:53.780 --> 00:32:56.000
I don't know that we
necessarily intended

00:32:56.000 --> 00:32:58.610
or thought that it would
end up that way, but it has.

00:32:58.610 --> 00:33:01.460
And there's not a day
that goes by where I'm not

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:04.760
talking to a startup
or investor that

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:07.580
is trying to better understand
how they can leverage those

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:11.720
to keep smaller teams, but
be able to scale infinitely.

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:15.890
And then a third open source
project out there, Apache Beam.

00:33:15.890 --> 00:33:19.730
So I won't go through a
massive amount of history.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:21.320
But what I will
say is if you think

00:33:21.320 --> 00:33:23.540
of the first MapReduce
papers that Google

00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:25.400
wrote, released into the wild.

00:33:25.400 --> 00:33:29.120
Someone took an interpretation
of that and created Hadoop.

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:32.180
And then we released more
papers and then Spark was born.

00:33:32.180 --> 00:33:35.750
So Hadoop for batch
processing, Spark for stream.

00:33:35.750 --> 00:33:39.710
So Apache Beam effectively
is combining that.

00:33:39.710 --> 00:33:43.850
And the hosted Google
Cloud version is data flow.

00:33:43.850 --> 00:33:47.240
And I always say
the magic of it is

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:51.290
you can do both batch
and stream in real time.

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:52.320
Amazing.

00:33:52.320 --> 00:33:55.790
But one of the trends that we
do see amongst a lot of startups

00:33:55.790 --> 00:33:59.420
is they are using Dataflow to
continuously keep models fed.

00:33:59.420 --> 00:34:01.404
And that's a very
powerful thing.

00:34:01.404 --> 00:34:02.945
So those of you that
aren't familiar,

00:34:02.945 --> 00:34:05.630
it might be a consideration.

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:08.840
So now from here
on out, I wanted

00:34:08.840 --> 00:34:12.199
to focus on a few
use cases of startups

00:34:12.199 --> 00:34:16.110
that they were born purely out
of use in machine learning.

00:34:16.110 --> 00:34:18.860
So [INAUDIBLE] is a startup.

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:22.969
And they are using
OCR, so Vision API.

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:24.830
And effectively as
you can see, they'll

00:34:24.830 --> 00:34:28.010
be able to take pictures
and be able to have

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:30.517
that be categorized with
some of the partners

00:34:30.517 --> 00:34:31.350
that they work with.

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:34.210
So large brand
retailers like Target.

00:34:34.210 --> 00:34:36.260
They even work with
Daimler Chrysler

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:40.820
to be able to take pictures of
their entire fleet of trucks

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:42.830
to be able to
understand which ones

00:34:42.830 --> 00:34:44.900
are in good condition,
which ones that are in

00:34:44.900 --> 00:34:46.429
need of repair.

00:34:46.429 --> 00:34:51.020
So that's a great example of
being able to leverage a Vision

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:54.590
API, but then also
for some things

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:57.770
that they might take pictures
of like labels and brands,

00:34:57.770 --> 00:35:01.460
then being able to use the
NLP API, natural language

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:03.290
processing to be
able to understand

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:07.290
blocks of text of what are
our customers saying about us?

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:11.590
And then how do we use
that to be able to iterate?

00:35:11.590 --> 00:35:16.030
Another one is a more
traditional industry.

00:35:16.030 --> 00:35:18.960
So farming-- so
in this case this

00:35:18.960 --> 00:35:22.230
is a Japanese cucumber farm.

00:35:22.230 --> 00:35:25.740
And you can think of
it in terms of it's

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:28.140
been a family business for ages.

00:35:28.140 --> 00:35:33.420
And son goes off to university
and prodigal son returns.

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:35.910
And his parents have always
taught him there's nothing

00:35:35.910 --> 00:35:38.160
more important than quality.

00:35:38.160 --> 00:35:42.150
But it was a laborious
manual process.

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:45.540
And everything had
to be done by hand

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:49.560
to sort out which ones are good
cucumbers, which ones are not.

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:54.330
And what he did is he got
a raspberry pie, hooked up

00:35:54.330 --> 00:35:59.040
a camera to it, download
a TensorFlow model locally

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:00.750
to the device.

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:04.920
And 7,000 pictures later, voila.

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:08.640
So pretty cool.

00:36:08.640 --> 00:36:10.640
And then the last one I
wanted to share with you

00:36:10.640 --> 00:36:13.250
is a company called Ravelin.

00:36:13.250 --> 00:36:18.500
So they are effectively helping
to solve for credit card fraud

00:36:18.500 --> 00:36:19.700
detection.

00:36:19.700 --> 00:36:21.530
So it's very cool
in how they're used

00:36:21.530 --> 00:36:22.880
in machine learning to do that.

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:24.421
So I'm going to play
a video for you.

00:36:28.424 --> 00:36:29.090
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:36:29.090 --> 00:36:32.230
- In addition to using machine
learning to catch fraudsters,

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:35.800
we use something called
network graph analysis.

00:36:35.800 --> 00:36:38.050
And on screen you'll see
a really simple network.

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:41.590
It consists of a single person
and a single credit card.

00:36:41.590 --> 00:36:43.750
Now don't be fooled by
that cute smiling face.

00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:46.954
That's actually a fraudster
and it's a stolen credit card.

00:36:46.954 --> 00:36:48.370
And if there was
another fraudster

00:36:48.370 --> 00:36:50.590
who used the same card, you'd
see them all linked together

00:36:50.590 --> 00:36:51.090
as well.

00:36:54.170 --> 00:36:56.860
So this is just a
more complex version

00:36:56.860 --> 00:36:59.140
of what you saw
previously, just with have

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:03.350
many more cards and many more
fraudsters all linked together.

00:37:03.350 --> 00:37:07.070
And I think it looks
pretty beautiful.

00:37:07.070 --> 00:37:13.010
And what you're looking at here
is an organized crime ring.

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:15.140
In this particular case,
we worked with the police

00:37:15.140 --> 00:37:16.610
and we gathered enough evidence.

00:37:16.610 --> 00:37:20.690
And we were able to put a
stop to the entire network.

00:37:20.690 --> 00:37:22.820
And we're particularly
proud of this

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:25.130
because it's a great
feeling knowing

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:27.840
that the technology
we built help

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:30.770
put a stop to
professional criminals.

00:37:30.770 --> 00:37:33.239
And this was the height
of our achievement

00:37:33.239 --> 00:37:34.655
until we came
across this network.

00:37:44.410 --> 00:37:47.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:38:04.410 --> 00:38:07.920
Internally we call
this the Death Star.

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:11.070
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:11.070 --> 00:38:14.670
There are tens of
thousands of nodes.

00:38:14.670 --> 00:38:17.580
And the most awesome thing
about networks of this size

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:20.250
is that when our machine
learning model catches

00:38:20.250 --> 00:38:22.560
a single fraudster,
because they're all

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:25.920
connected in a network, we
can put a stop to thousands

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:27.540
of accounts instantly.

00:38:27.540 --> 00:38:29.020
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:33.590
So wonderful work Lenny and
the team at Ravelin are doing.

00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:35.110
But this hopefully
should give you

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:39.580
a semblance of a very
real world problem, fraud,

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:40.900
that exists out there.

00:38:40.900 --> 00:38:45.280
And obviously, it's wonderful
to see the kind of social impact

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:48.146
that they have, but using
world class technology

00:38:48.146 --> 00:38:49.270
to be able to achieve that.

00:38:52.050 --> 00:38:55.730
So I always provide
a bit of guidance,

00:38:55.730 --> 00:39:00.420
because there is this
kind of pent up demand

00:39:00.420 --> 00:39:04.100
and talk about AI
and machine learning.

00:39:04.100 --> 00:39:08.000
And the reality is, we need to
start thinking about in terms

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:11.120
of it's own category.

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:12.920
The truth is that
it's going to be

00:39:12.920 --> 00:39:16.400
a fabric of every company
that exists going forward.

00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:20.830
Now some companies, it might
be very core to what they do.

00:39:20.830 --> 00:39:23.420
Others it just might
be an ancillary piece

00:39:23.420 --> 00:39:25.040
that they add in
because it might

00:39:25.040 --> 00:39:27.200
be able to somehow move
the needle in how they're

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:29.180
able to delight customers.

00:39:29.180 --> 00:39:33.440
But I do urge everyone to
consider that they do it

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:35.480
for the right
reasons, whether it's

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:40.880
because you want to change
humanity, want to be a world

00:39:40.880 --> 00:39:43.220
beating company and
create a new category,

00:39:43.220 --> 00:39:45.080
or become a category leader.

00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:48.360
And certainly from a startup
perspective, I always say,

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:50.870
don't do because you want to
raise some crazy valuation

00:39:50.870 --> 00:39:53.420
or for an acqui-hire,
because ultimately, you'll

00:39:53.420 --> 00:39:55.170
be setting yourself
up for failure.

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:57.890
But do it for the right
reasons, because it's very easy

00:39:57.890 --> 00:40:03.920
to get caught up in this buzz
word bingo and all the nonsense

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:04.730
that comes with it.

00:40:08.690 --> 00:40:11.480
As I said, it will
exist, and it will

00:40:11.480 --> 00:40:14.150
be a part of businesses
going forward.

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:17.390
But it's very important
to take into consideration

00:40:17.390 --> 00:40:22.100
about everything
today is about speed.

00:40:22.100 --> 00:40:23.270
And it's about velocity.

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:26.810
And I love that word
velocity, because velocity

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:30.560
encompasses two main
things, speed and direction.

00:40:30.560 --> 00:40:32.150
So it's ultimately
trying to make sure

00:40:32.150 --> 00:40:33.530
that you understand
where you're going,

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:35.240
and you're doing it
at the right speed.

00:40:35.240 --> 00:40:37.670
But the ability to
do that is going

00:40:37.670 --> 00:40:40.550
to be about the underlying
technology that you use,

00:40:40.550 --> 00:40:44.600
and always keeping the customer
at the forefront of what

00:40:44.600 --> 00:40:45.510
you're going to do.

00:40:45.510 --> 00:40:49.350
So I'll leave you with that
and I appreciate your time.

00:40:49.350 --> 00:40:50.016
Thank you.

